[[:db/retract 17592186075118 :answer-field/choices 17592186075120] [:db/retract 17592186075118 :answer-field/choices 17592186075121] [:db/retract 17592186075118 :answer-field/choices 17592186075122] [:db/retract 17592186075118 :answer-field/choices 17592186075123] [:db/retract 17592186075118 :answer-field/choices 17592186075119] {:db/id 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0c85f83e-b227-560c-a6a8-ac2b41f8a28a" "question-daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" "question-4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078374 :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c85f83e-b227-560c-a6a8-ac2b41f8a28a" :question/id #uuid "0c85f83e-b227-560c-a6a8-ac2b41f8a28a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106656" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=5,a=90,$ and ${y}_{0}=130.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=60,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=60<a=90.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d452b5a-2fc4-539e-b756-0fb969fcb4bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d452b5a-2fc4-539e-b756-0fb969fcb4bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d452b5a-2fc4-539e-b756-0fb969fcb4bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" "answer-5d1b5621-3f57-51c5-806e-e99b647b764b" "answer-4815316b-c603-51a2-a732-458284f7c774" "answer-e9fd2ab7-1578-5858-845a-20b1a7944785" "answer-e4e26874-dc7d-53de-91b5-91950a6ae0a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" :answer/id #uuid "2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d1b5621-3f57-51c5-806e-e99b647b764b" :answer/id #uuid "5d1b5621-3f57-51c5-806e-e99b647b764b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4815316b-c603-51a2-a732-458284f7c774" :answer/id #uuid "4815316b-c603-51a2-a732-458284f7c774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y-90),\\,y(0)=60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9fd2ab7-1578-5858-845a-20b1a7944785" :answer/id #uuid "e9fd2ab7-1578-5858-845a-20b1a7944785" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4e26874-dc7d-53de-91b5-91950a6ae0a7" :answer/id #uuid "e4e26874-dc7d-53de-91b5-91950a6ae0a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" :question/id #uuid "daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106652" :question/problem "Given that $y>16$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-60(y-16).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=60,$ and $a=16.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>16$ for all $t>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33d8e051-9b26-56c6-8fc8-5f8fb9709d58"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33d8e051-9b26-56c6-8fc8-5f8fb9709d58" :answer-field/id #uuid "33d8e051-9b26-56c6-8fc8-5f8fb9709d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" :answer-field/choices ["answer-37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" "answer-c269d59b-4c24-5a2e-8b68-d56b249ca9ca" "answer-3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" "answer-c77bbc7e-9d55-561a-80a6-ebb8b2046097" "answer-fd079cb4-c93e-5568-a211-4982fc99b62f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" :answer/id #uuid "37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=60{y}^{2}-960" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c269d59b-4c24-5a2e-8b68-d56b249ca9ca" :answer/id #uuid "c269d59b-4c24-5a2e-8b68-d56b249ca9ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960+60y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" :answer/id #uuid "3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c77bbc7e-9d55-561a-80a6-ebb8b2046097" :answer/id #uuid "c77bbc7e-9d55-561a-80a6-ebb8b2046097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd079cb4-c93e-5568-a211-4982fc99b62f" :answer/id #uuid "fd079cb4-c93e-5568-a211-4982fc99b62f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960y-60{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1" :question/id #uuid "4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106658" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=30,a=200,$ and ${y}_{0}=210.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=100<a=200.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85c16440-4f36-5799-80af-9e7a27fdee13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85c16440-4f36-5799-80af-9e7a27fdee13" :answer-field/id #uuid "85c16440-4f36-5799-80af-9e7a27fdee13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7de9a5d8-fca6-54df-be6c-1bc3b035ed8b" "answer-11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" "answer-b5f10bfc-7725-5838-829c-638f4fde25b9" "answer-0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" "answer-cddd42ed-edf0-54e7-998a-1f3cc455ccb0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7de9a5d8-fca6-54df-be6c-1bc3b035ed8b" :answer/id #uuid "7de9a5d8-fca6-54df-be6c-1bc3b035ed8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" :answer/id #uuid "11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5f10bfc-7725-5838-829c-638f4fde25b9" :answer/id #uuid "b5f10bfc-7725-5838-829c-638f4fde25b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30y(y-200),\\,y(0)=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" :answer/id #uuid "0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cddd42ed-edf0-54e7-998a-1f3cc455ccb0" :answer/id #uuid "cddd42ed-edf0-54e7-998a-1f3cc455ccb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30y(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3" :question/id #uuid "502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106932" :question/problem "The rate at which a cup of chocolate cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the chocolate in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-16).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08edadb3-2531-58ae-aec8-c90d8179de19"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08edadb3-2531-58ae-aec8-c90d8179de19" :answer-field/id #uuid "08edadb3-2531-58ae-aec8-c90d8179de19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6056756e-9986-54df-b147-e00ce576b435" "answer-5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" "answer-61ae82ec-9879-5f5d-ab92-a1997ae823f6" "answer-42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" "answer-f46e95ee-3654-530d-b86d-938504c88cb1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6056756e-9986-54df-b147-e00ce576b435" :answer/id #uuid "6056756e-9986-54df-b147-e00ce576b435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" :answer/id #uuid "5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61ae82ec-9879-5f5d-ab92-a1997ae823f6" :answer/id #uuid "61ae82ec-9879-5f5d-ab92-a1997ae823f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" :answer/id #uuid "42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f46e95ee-3654-530d-b86d-938504c88cb1" :answer/id #uuid "f46e95ee-3654-530d-b86d-938504c88cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-16)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078707 :question/problem "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075118 :answer-field/correct "answer-72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" "answer-a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" "answer-ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" "answer-e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" "answer-e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/id #uuid "72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" :answer/id #uuid "a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" :answer/id #uuid "ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" :answer/id #uuid "e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346" :answer/id #uuid "e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079671 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n*The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079619 :tutorial/content "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078671 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
